The heuristic `existing.createdAt.includes('T') && existing.createdAt.endsWith('Z')` to detect valid ISO strings will incorrectly reject valid ISO 8601 timestamps with timezone offsets (e.g., `2024-01-01T10:00:00+00:00` or `2024-01-01T10:00:00.000+01:00`). While the codebase currently generates only `Z`-suffixed dates via `toISOString()`, this could silently convert and re-save a valid ISO string with a timezone offset, potentially shifting the timestamp. A more robust approach would be to attempt parsing with a regex like `/^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}T/` or using `!isNaN(Date.parse(existing.createdAt))` combined with a format check.
```suggestion
        // If it's not a valid ISO 8601 string, convert it.
        const rawCreatedAt: unknown = existing.createdAt as unknown;
        let sanitizedCreatedAt: string;
        if (rawCreatedAt instanceof Date) {
            sanitizedCreatedAt = rawCreatedAt.toISOString();
        } else if (typeof rawCreatedAt === "string") {
            const looksIsoLike = /^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}T/.test(rawCreatedAt);
            const canParse = !Number.isNaN(Date.parse(rawCreatedAt));
            sanitizedCreatedAt = looksIsoLike && canParse
                ? rawCreatedAt
                : new Date(rawCreatedAt).toISOString();
        } else {
            sanitizedCreatedAt = new Date(rawCreatedAt as any).toISOString();
        }
```

